Part 1 of canlin#40 (LLM step 3, providers spec §10.3). The mechanism, not the product: the privileged binding a module with the assistant-view capability uses. The module-roles spec §3 names the capability 'assistant-view' and says it gives 'the model-view binding (set_view rendering, submissions)', default for modules with role 'assistant', and that a capability that is not granted makes the API absent (nil) or answers denied - never a pretend success. The C pieces exist: canlin_assistant_view (validated model holder, canlin_assistant_view_set_view, _attach, _input, _draw) and canlin_assistant_response (the gate: _begin, _text, _tool, _finish, _finish_length, _cancel, _outcome). What is missing is the binding itself and its install. Notes: the install mechanism already exists - canlin_module_source.assistant is a trusted loader callback (include/canlin/module.h:101), and canlin_llm_install (src/llm/llm.c:1363) is the shape to follow; nothing sets that callback yet, so part 2 wires it. The security question to answer in the report: what a hostile module can do with the binding, and why the answer is bounded (the sandbox is the security boundary). The spec's own rule for a failed or cut-off answer (llm-ui design §4, decided 2026-09-24): keep the text marked 'abgebrochen' or 'gekürzt', record it in the history without the dropped tool calls, leave the view unchanged and say so in one short line.

Binding built in src/assistant/binding.c (new) with include/canlin/assistant_binding.h (new); the model-view submission as a Lua table comes from view.c (canlin_assistant_view_take_lua, new). Lua surface: canlin.view.draw() -> revision (drawn into the module's own view) and canlin.view.submission() -> {revision=, action=, values={}} (taken). The install is the trusted assistant hook of canlin_module_source: canlin_assistant_binding_install(L, m, ud), ud = the loader-owned canlin_assistant_binding {view, granted}. Not granted: the loader does not call the install, so canlin.view is nil (absent); revoked: granted = 0 and every answer is nil, 'denied', <message>. The binding takes no ui argument: it pushes the module's own view, so a module cannot draw into another view. Failure/cut-off is NOT carried by the binding: the done event already carries ok and result={stop, dropped_tools}, and the response gate drops the tool calls of a failed turn in C, so the view is unchanged by construction and the module learns everything from the event. One behaviour change in the existing C: a replaced view now drops its pending submission (its values are gone with that view) - the revision rule in one place.

Two findings for part 2 (k109, platform lane), noted here, not fixed here. (1) The set_view intake and the tool result are not in the binding, on purpose: only the response gate may dispatch a set_view, and only after the turn ended ok (LLM providers 2.3). A module-facing set_view would let a module forge a model view. So the loader must feed the 'llm' tool events into canlin_assistant_response and own the gate; the module then needs the tool result ({revision} or the structured error) to send back to the provider - the gate holds it in C today (canlin_assistant_response_call(i).res) and no module-facing API carries it. That is the conversation layer's business (k109), not the view binding's. (2) The model view's widgets and the module's own widgets share the ui view's identity space ('r<rev>:<id>'): a module that draws a widget with a colliding identity in an earlier frame can prime the model view's field/choice state. It stays inside the module's own view (the shell frames the module's view as the model view), so it reaches nothing new, but a per-view nonce in the identity would close it - a change to the identity scheme, so a ticket, not scope creep here.

Done: the assistant-view binding and its install. New: include/canlin/assistant_binding.h (canlin_assistant_binding {view, granted}, canlin_assistant_binding_install) and src/assistant/binding.c (the Lua surface); src/assistant/view.c gains canlin_assistant_view_take_lua (the submission's JSON as a Lua table) and a replaced view now drops its pending submission (the revision rule in one place); t/assistant/test_binding.c is the new suite (the module runs in the real sandbox: it draws the model view into its own view and picks up the submission), t/assistant/test_view.c gains the dropped-submission case; mk/ui_assistant.mk builds the new object and the new suite. Lua surface: canlin.view.draw() -> the revision drawn (into the module's own view, no ui argument, so a module cannot draw elsewhere) and canlin.view.submission() -> {revision=, action=, values={}}, taken. Not granted: the loader does not call the install, so canlin.view is nil; revoked: granted = 0 and every answer is nil, 'denied', <message>. Verified: make BUILD=build/llm test passed (ALL TESTS PASSED) before another lane's in-progress edit to t/module/test_module.c broke that file's build; after that edit the 17 other suites were rebuilt and run green, including assistant view 1922, assistant response 3582, assistant binding 65 checks, and make SAN=asan BUILD=build/llm test passed (ALL SANITIZER TESTS PASSED). The module suite could not be rebuilt at the end: t/module/test_module.c has an unused static function (another lane's work in progress, not mine) - it is named here, not implied green.

